Licence notice for furios_gps
=============================

Everything in this repository that we wrote is MIT (see LICENSE): gpsctl, the
proxy, the units, the polkit action, the installer, the tests, the packaging.
Every one of those files carries an SPDX header saying so.

There is one file here we did not write:

    original-files/geoclue.conf

It is geoclue's own configuration file, as the geoclue-2.0 package installs it
on FuriOS, kept byte for byte. It is here for two reasons: the tests need a
real file to work on rather than a fixture somebody invented, and `gpsctl
revert` needs to know what the shipped values were. It is not modified, not
patched, and not installed over anybody's copy - the .deb puts it under
/usr/share/furios-gps-fix/ and never into /etc, because geoclue.conf is
geoclue's conffile and a second package writing one is how a package upgrade
starts asking people which version of a file they want.

geoclue is GPL-2.0-or-later, Copyright the geoclue authors.

Nothing here is a derived work of geoclue's code. The change this project makes
to geoclue is to two values in its configuration file, at runtime, on the
machine it is installed on - which is what a configuration file is for.

The proxy itself uses nothing but the Python standard library. One tool
beside it, tools/ask-geoclue.py, uses GLib and Gio (LGPL-2.1+) through
PyGObject (LGPL-2.1+) - it has to, because geoclue ties a client to the D-Bus
connection that asked for it, so single gdbus calls measure nothing. It is a
diagnostic tool, not part of what gets installed.

The geolocation service the proxy talks to is BeaconDB (https://beacondb.net),
a public instance speaking ichnaea's API. This project is not affiliated with
it and ships no data from it.

Until now the sentence here also said that nothing is sent which geoclue was
not going to send anyway. That is still true of the proxy, and no longer true
of the whole repository: tools/furios-gps-contribute submits Wi-Fi
observations to beaconDB's geosubmit endpoint.

It is off unless somebody turns it on, and when it is on it sends the MAC
addresses, channels and signal strengths of the networks in range together
with a GNSS position - never the network names, never anything hidden, and
never a network whose name ends in _nomap or _optout, which is how the owner
of an access point says no. Those are beaconDB's own rules; the ones about
sending only over Wi-Fi, only from a satellite fix, and rarely are ours.

What that means for the people around you: the access points of neighbours and
shops are part of what gets sent, because that is what a public geolocation
database is made of. beaconDB obfuscates published data so that a location can
only be retrieved by somebody who already observed the same network, and it
delays publishing a moved access point for a year. Anyone who does not want to
be in it adds _nomap to their SSID, and this tool honours that.
